"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings show a Gleason 4+5, pT3a stage, negative surgical margins, non-invasion of seminal vesicles, absence of lymph node metastasis, and absence of lymphovascular invasion. These favorable pathological features suggest a lower risk profile. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 12 and the high CAPRA-S score of 6.0. The biopsy grade (Gleason 0+0) differs significantly from the prostatectomy specimen (Gleason 4+5), which introduces uncertainty regarding the true extent of the disease. Nodal status is documented as negative (no lymph node metastasis).\n\nInformation that would revise this prognosis significantly includes the postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. A rising postoperative PSA would be a key indicator of potential recurrence. Since postoperative PSA and follow-up are not documented, this remains a critical unknown. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence, but the trajectory is dependent on future clinical data. The fixed estimated horizon is 85.63 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 68.50–102.76 months; its coverage has not been validated."
